Reason:
<text>

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
iph = skb_put(skb, sizeof(struct iphdr));
iph->protocol = IPPROTO_UDP; iph->saddr = in_aton("192.0.2.1"); iph->daddr = in_aton("198.51.100.1");
Reason:
`skb_put` only moves the tail pointer without zeroing the allocated memory. After this, only the `protocol`, `saddr`, and `daddr` fields of the IP header are explicitly set, leaving fields like `version`, `ihl`, `tos`, `ttl`, `id`, `frag_off`, `check`, etc. uninitialized. When the sk_buff is later processed (e.g., by KMSAN), these uninitialized bytes trigger a warning.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
iph = skb_put_zero(skb, sizeof(struct iphdr));
iph->version = 0x4; iph->frag_off = 0; iph->ihl = 0x5; iph->tot_len = htons(tot_len); iph->ttl = 100; iph->check = ip_fast_csum((unsigned char *)iph, iph->ihl);
Reason:
`skb_put_zero` zero-initializes the entire IP header allocation, ensuring no uninitialized bytes remain. Additionally, all fields (including those previously omitted) are now explicitly set to valid values, further guaranteeing correct behavior and avoiding any uninit-value warnings.

